in late 2011 hundreds of namibians took  to the tarmac at vendek's airport to  receive remains of their ancestors the  country had endured a long wait these  skulls belonged to the indigenous Herero  and nama who rebelled against german  colonizers and were brutally killed a  century ago the skulls had been housed  the world away in German museums ever  since back then the imperialists called  the land German Southwest Africa  present-day namibia in 1904 the Germans  captured thousands of ethnic Herrero  during the Battle of vato Beck they  forced them into the oume Hecky desert  and sealed it off the prisoners died of  hunger and thirst an estimated 100,000  Herrero were killed fighting the Germans  100 years later a representative of the  German government travelled to Namibia  to deliver the first official apology I  ask you to forgive us our trespasses  precipices and our guilt many say it's  too early to celebrate no reparations  have been made or approved the German  government had refused to label the  Herero Massacre a genocide because the  term was not legally defined until 1948  yet the German parliament recently said  the killing of one and a half million  Armenians by the Turks in 1915 was a  genocide now norbert lammott the  president of the parliament says the  suppression of the Herero rebellion was  genocide - that will increase the  pressure for Germany to take greater  responsibility for its colonial past
 

